Viability, Reachability, and the Topology of Civilizational State Space: A Formal Research Program

ouadi Maakoul 

AI: chatGpt, Deepseek, Meta AI , Gemini 


Abstract

This work develops a formal framework for analyzing structural-demographic stress and civilizational collapse as a problem in viability theory, dynamical reachability, and topological data analysis. The central formal distinction is between the viable set — states satisfying constraint inequalities — and the reachable viable set — viable states dynamically accessible from the historical present under bounded institutional control. Collapse is defined as an eigenvalue crossing of the linearized dynamics at a parameterized equilibrium family, not as an undefined graph derivative. The framework treats the trust threshold τ* as a domain-specific prior to be calibrated, not a universal constant. The Seshat Global History Databank, coding 77 social-complexity variables across 620 polities spanning approximately 10,000 years, provides the empirical substrate for calibration, with explicit protocols for missing-data imputation, temporal-resolution sensitivity, and null-model testing for persistent homology. The framework generates six falsifiable research programs and specifies failure conditions. The normative register — including the human–AI authority constraint — is never derived from the formal or empirical registers. The topology of the reachable viable set is a structural fact about the accessible future under a given model and constraint specification, not a normative prescription.

