1. Introduction

1.1 The Problem of Civilizational Viability

The question of which civilizational trajectories remain viable under current structural-demographic conditions is not merely empirical. It is formal. A state can satisfy every constraint inequality we might impose — resource ceilings, inequality bounds, trust floors — and yet be dynamically inaccessible from the present under any admissible institutional control. Conversely, a state may be dynamically accessible and yet violate the constraints we choose to impose. The distinction between the viable set and the reachable viable set is the formal core of this work.

Viability theory, founded by Aubin, provides the mathematical apparatus for this distinction. The viability kernel $\mathrm{Viab}(K)$ is the set of initial states from which at least one evolution remains within the constraint set $K$ for all future times. This kernel is the maximal closed viability domain contained in $K$. It is the set of states that can survive — not merely the set of states that satisfy inequalities at a moment in time.

Reachability theory provides the complementary apparatus. A state may be in the viability kernel and yet be unreachable from the historical present under bounded control. Maidens et al. established that the connection between reachability and viability enables computation of the viability kernel using reach sets, with Lagrangian methods — ellipsoidal and support-vector set representations — scaling to dimensions previously infeasible for Eulerian methods. Their computational complexity is polynomial in time and space, making them suitable for high-dimensional systems. This work exploits that connection.

1.2 Structural-Demographic Stress and Critical Transitions

Structural-demographic theory, developed by Turchin and colleagues, identifies elite overproduction, popular immiseration, and declining state legitimacy as drivers of political instability. The Seshat Global History Databank, coding 77 social-complexity variables across 620 polities, provides the empirical substrate for testing such theories at century-scale resolution. Quantitative analysis of Seshat data has uncovered a single dimension of complexity that structures global variation in human social organization.

The formal question is how to characterize the transition from a viable to a non-viable configuration. This work defines collapse as an eigenvalue crossing of the linearized dynamics:

\Re \lambda_{\max}(J_F(x_c)) = 0

at a parameterized equilibrium family $F(x^*(\mu), \mu) = 0$. The crossing of the dominant eigenvalue through zero indicates loss of stability, through a Hopf bifurcation or saddle-node bifurcation depending on the eigenvalue structure. Early warning signals — rising variance and lag-1 autocorrelation — are predicted by critical slowing down near such bifurcations, though false positives are elevated and the joint criterion must be applied carefully.

The transition may be continuous or discontinuous. To establish a first-order transition, this work requires at least one of: hysteresis, bistability, an order-parameter jump, or cusp catastrophe geometry. Cusp-type hysteresis emerges from the combination of heterogeneous thresholds and positive feedback, generating bistability and history-dependent switching between states. The cusp potential $V(\psi) = \psi^4/4 - \mu\psi^2/2 - h\psi$ provides the canonical model.

1.3 Topological Structure of the Accessible Future

The unexplored reachable set $\mathcal{X}_{\text{unexplored}}$ consists of viable states reachable from the historical present but not present in the historical record. Its topology — connected components, loops, voids — characterizes the shape of the accessible future. A single connected component suggests a corridor of viable trajectories; multiple components suggest separated basins; loops suggest cyclic attractors; voids suggest inaccessible regions surrounded by viable states.

Persistent homology provides the computational apparatus for extracting this topology. The Vietoris–Rips filtration $\mathrm{VR}_r = \{\sigma \subseteq \mathcal{X}_{\text{unexplored}} : \mathrm{diam}(\sigma) \le r\}$ yields persistence diagrams $D_0, D_1, D_2$, computed via Ripser or comparable libraries. Crucially, a persistence diagram is not a property of the data alone. It depends on the construction, the null model, and the autocorrelation of the filtration values. Bootstrap methods provide confidence bands for persistence diagrams and significance tests for topological features. Significance is assessed against pre-registered null models, including matched-density Poisson processes, block bootstrap preserving temporal autocorrelation, and phase-randomized surrogates.

The topological signature of $\mathcal{X}_{\text{unexplored}}$ is a structural fact about the reachable viable set under a given model and constraint specification. It is not a normative prescription. The mapping from topology to dynamics is established through the estimated dynamics $\hat{F}$, not assumed. A persistent $H_1$ feature means the data contain a robust loop-like structure under the chosen filtration; it does not automatically imply a stable periodic orbit.

1.4 The Normative Register

The constraint $H_{\text{auth}}(t) \ge H_{\text{crit}}$, where $H_{\text{auth}}$ denotes human–AI authority, is normative. It is a chosen boundary condition, not a theorem. The is–ought gap establishes that normative statements cannot be logically derived from descriptive premises. The normative register is exogenous to the formal and empirical registers. Different values of $H_{\text{crit}}$ carve different viability kernels. The volume of the viability kernel as a function of $H_{\text{crit}}$ is reported as a sensitivity analysis.

This separation is not a stylistic preference. It is a logical necessity. A framework that derives normative constraints from formal or empirical premises commits a category error. The formal apparatus can tell us what is reachable under a given constraint specification. It cannot tell us which constraints to choose.

