Failure Conditions

The framework fails if the order-parameter description is not predictive (R2), collapse events are not preceded by eigenvalue crossings or early warning signals (R3), the topology of $\mathcal{X}_{\text{unexplored}}$ is indistinguishable from null models (R6), or the normative constraint $H_{\text{auth}} \ge H_{\text{crit}}$ cannot be operationalized without importing unstated political assumptions.

11. Human Possibility Space

The formal object $\mathcal{X}_{\text{unexplored}}$ is the set of reachable viable states not present in the historical record. It is the Human Possibility Space in the technical sense: not all conceivable futures, but those that are (a) viable under the chosen constraints and (b) dynamically reachable from the present.

The topology of $\mathcal{X}_{\text{unexplored}}$—its connected components, loops, and voids—characterizes the shape of the accessible future. A single connected component suggests a corridor of viable trajectories; multiple components suggest separated basins; loops suggest cyclic attractors; voids suggest inaccessible regions surrounded by viable states.

These topological features are not normative prescriptions. They are structural facts about the reachable viable set under a given model and constraint specification. The normative question—which regions of $\mathcal{X}_{\text{unexplored}}$ should be pursued—is answered in the normative register, not derived from topology.

12. Formal, Empirical, and Normative Separation

