The operationalization of $H_{\text{auth}}$ requires a measurable proxy: fraction of executive decisions requiring human signature, or human override rate in AI-assisted governance. Without operationalization, the constraint cannot be varied in sensitivity analysis. $H_{\text{auth}}$ is an exogenous political choice; different $H_{\text{crit}}$ values carve different viability kernels. The volume of $\text{Viab}$ as a function of $H_{\text{crit}}$ is reported.

13. Conclusion

The architecture developed here replaces an ambitious but under-specified claim with a rigorous research program: estimate dynamics from historical data, define viability constraints, compute the reachable viable set, and characterize its topology with proper null models and stability analysis. The central formal distinction

\Omega_{\text{viable}} \neq \Omega_{\text{reachable}}

is stronger than earlier formulations because it allows us to ask which viable futures are actually accessible from the present. The framework is falsifiable at every stage. The trust threshold is a domain-specific prior, not a universal constant. The topology of the Human Possibility Space is a structural fact about the reachable viable set, not a normative prescription. The skeleton survives; it is now a mathematical research program.


Appendices

Appendix A: Proofs

A.1 Closure of the Viability Kernel

Theorem A.1 (Aubin). Let K \subseteq \mathbb{R}^d be closed, and let F: \mathbb{R}^d \rightrightarrows \mathbb{R}^d be an upper semicontinuous set-valued map with nonempty compact convex images satisfying the linear growth condition \sup_{v \in F(x)} \|v\| \le k(\|x\|+1). Then the viability kernel \mathrm{Viab}(K) is closed.

Proof. The viability kernel is the set of initial conditions x_0 \in K from which at least one solution of the differential inclusion \dot{x}(t) \in F(x(t)) exists over \mathbb{R}_+ and remains in K for all t \ge 0. Under the stated hypotheses, the solution map is upper semicontinuous with compact values, and the set of viable trajectories is closed in the compact-open topology. The evaluation map at t=0 is continuous, so the preimage of K under the viability relation is closed. Hence \mathrm{Viab}(K) is closed. ∎

The closure property is essential for numerical approximation: any convergent sequence of states in the viability kernel has its limit in the kernel, so the kernel can be approximated from within by nested closed sets.

A.2 Strict Inclusion of the Reachable Viable Set

Theorem A.2. Let \Omega_{\text{viable}} be the set of trajectories satisfying all constraint inequalities, and let \Omega_{\text{reachable}} \subseteq \Omega_{\text{viable}} be the set of viable trajectories starting from the historical tube \mathcal{T}_H under admissible control u \in U. If the forward reachable set from \mathcal{T}_H does not cover \mathcal{S}_{\text{viable}}, then the inclusion is strict:

\Omega_{\text{reachable}} \subsetneq \Omega_{\text{viable}}.

Proof. By definition, \Omega_{\text{reachable}} \subseteq \Omega_{\text{viable}}. Suppose for contradiction that \Omega_{\text{reachable}} = \Omega_{\text{viable}}. Then every viable trajectory \gamma \in \Omega_{\text{viable}} must satisfy \gamma(0) \in \mathcal{T}_H. But \Omega_{\text{viable}} contains trajectories starting from any point in \mathcal{S}_{\text{viable}} that admit a viable continuation. If the forward reachable set from \mathcal{T}_H does not cover \mathcal{S}_{\text{viable}}, there exists x_0 \in \mathcal{S}_{\text{viable}} with x_0 \notin \mathcal{T}_H and no admissible control u transporting x_0 into the reachable set from \mathcal{T}_H. Any viable trajectory starting at x_0 is in \Omega_{\text{viable}} but not in \Omega_{\text{reachable}}. Contradiction. ∎

This theorem formalizes the central distinction: the viable set is an algebraic object (states satisfying inequalities), while the reachable viable set is a dynamical object (states accessible under bounded control from a given historical condition).

A.3 Cheeger Bound for the Spectral Gap

Theorem A.3 (Cheeger). Let G be an undirected graph with normalized Laplacian L = I - D^{-1/2} A D^{-1/2} and eigenvalues 0 = \lambda_1 \le \lambda_2 \le \dots \le \lambda_n \le 2. Let h(G) be the Cheeger constant (minimum conductance). Then:

\frac{\lambda_2}{2} \le h(G) \le \sqrt{2\lambda_2}.

Proof. The lower bound follows from the variational characterization of \lambda_2 and the definition of conductance. The upper bound is proved by constructing a sweep cut from the Fiedler vector and bounding the conductance of the cut by \sqrt{2\lambda_2}. ∎

The Cheeger inequality provides the analytic link between the spectral gap \lambda_2 and modular fragmentation. As \lambda_2 \to 0, the graph becomes disconnected, and the Cheeger constant approaches zero. The fragmentation threshold is not \lambda_2 = 0 exactly but \lambda_2 < \lambda_{\text{crit}}, where algebraic connectivity is insufficient for integration given elite pressure.

A.4 Eigenvalue Crossing Implies Loss of Stability

Theorem A.4. Let \dot{x} = F(x, \mu) with equilibrium family F(x^*(\mu), \mu) = 0. Suppose the Jacobian J(\mu) = D_x F(x^*(\mu), \mu) has a simple eigenvalue \lambda(\mu) with \Re \lambda(\mu_c) = 0 and \frac{d}{d\mu} \Re \lambda(\mu) \big|_{\mu_c} > 0. Then the equilibrium x^*(\mu) loses stability at \mu_c.

Proof. By the implicit function theorem, the equilibrium family persists as long as J(\mu) is invertible. The eigenvalues of J(\mu) vary continuously with \mu. For \mu < \mu_c, \Re \lambda(\mu) < 0, so the equilibrium is stable. For \mu > \mu_c, \Re \lambda(\mu) > 0, so the equilibrium is unstable. The crossing \Re \lambda(\mu_c) = 0 is therefore the critical threshold. ∎

A.5 Filippov Well-Posedness via Sigmoid Smoothing

Theorem A.5. Let S_\varepsilon: \mathbb{R} \to (0,1) be a sigmoid function with S_\varepsilon(z) \to H(z) pointwise as \varepsilon \to 0, where H is the Heaviside function. Then the smoothed system \dot{x} = F(x) S_\varepsilon(g(x)) + G(x)(1 - S_\varepsilon(g(x))) converges uniformly on finite time intervals to a Filippov solution of the discontinuous system as \varepsilon \to 0.

Proof. The sigmoid S_\varepsilon is Lipschitz continuous for each \varepsilon > 0, so the smoothed vector field is Lipschitz and the smoothed system has a unique solution. As \varepsilon \to 0, the vector field converges pointwise to the discontinuous vector field. By the Filippov convexification theorem, the limit of solutions of the smoothed system satisfies the differential inclusion \dot{x} \in \mathrm{co}\{F(x), G(x)\} on the switching manifold. Uniform convergence on finite intervals follows from Grönwall's inequality and the boundedness of the vector field. ∎

Appendix B: Computational Algorithms

B.1 Subset Simulation for Rare-Event Viability Volume

Subset simulation decomposes a small failure probability into a product of larger conditional probabilities by introducing intermediate failure events. For the viability kernel computation in k \approx 5\text{–}10 dimensions, define the target event F = \{x \in \mathcal{S}_{\text{viable}} : x \notin \mathrm{Viab}(\mathcal{S}_{\text{viable}})\}. The algorithm proceeds by:

1. Generate N samples from the prior distribution over \mathcal{S}_{\text{viable}}.
2. Evaluate the viability indicator for each sample.
3. Set the first intermediate threshold F_1 as the p_0-quantile of the viability indicator, with p_0 = 0.1.
4. Generate samples conditional on F_1 using Markov Chain Monte Carlo.
5. Repeat until the target event is reached.

The failure probability is estimated as P(F) = P(F_1) \prod_{j=2}^{L} P(F_j \mid F_{j-1}), with each conditional probability estimated from the conditional samples. This approach avoids the curse of dimensionality that afflicts naive Monte Carlo when the viable volume is small.

B.2 Ellipsoidal Reachability for Viability Kernel Approximation

For linear dynamics \dot{x} = Ax + Bu with convex state and input constraints, the viability kernel can be computed via backward reachable sets:

S_{n+1} = K \cap \mathrm{Reach}_{\text{bwd}}(S_n).

Ellipsoidal set representations approximate these sets as \mathcal{E}(c, Q) = \{x : (x-c)^\top Q^{-1} (x-c) \le 1\}. The backward reachability operator for ellipsoids is:

\mathrm{Reach}_{\text{bwd}}(\mathcal{E}(c, Q)) = \mathcal{E}(c', Q'),

where c' = e^{-A \Delta t} c and Q' = e^{-A \Delta t} Q e^{-A^\top \Delta t} + \int_0^{\Delta t} e^{-A s} B U B^\top e^{-A^\top s} ds. The intersection of ellipsoids is outer-approximated by the minimal-volume ellipsoid containing the intersection. The algorithm scales polynomially in state dimension, with complexity O(N_{\text{iter}} \cdot N_{\text{ell}} \cdot k^3).

B.3 Weak-Form SINDy and Identifiability Conditions

Weak-form SINDy replaces pointwise differentiation with integral constraints:

\int_{t_i}^{t_{i+1}} \dot{x}(t) \phi(t) dt = \int_{t_i}^{t_{i+1}} \Theta(x(t)) \Xi \phi(t) dt,

where \phi are test functions and \Theta(x) is the library of candidate terms. The noise contribution to the weak-form regression matrix scales as O(\sigma_\eta^2 \Delta t), which decreases rather than diverges as \Delta t \to 0.

Three conditions are required for unique recovery of the true sparse coefficient vectors: (i) the library must be rich enough to span the true dynamics; (ii) the trajectory data must provide sufficient coverage of the state space, formally the design matrix must have full column rank; and (iii) the LASSO support recovery requires an irrepresentability condition, which is mitigated by column normalization and STLSQ refinement.

B.4 Bottleneck Bootstrap for Persistence Diagrams

Given a point cloud \mathcal{X} and its persistence diagram D, generate B bootstrap resamples \mathcal{X}^{(b)} and compute D^{(b)}. The bottleneck distance d_B(D^{(b)}, D) quantifies the stability of each topological feature. Features with d_B below the 1-\alpha quantile of the bootstrap distribution are retained as significant. Confidence bands for persistence landscapes are constructed by the same procedure.

Appendix C: Data Protocol

C.1 Seshat Structure and Missingness Mechanism

Seshat codes 77 social-complexity variables across 620 polities at century-scale resolution. Missing data is pervasive and non-random. The missingness mechanism is tested using Little's test per natural geographic area. For variables where MNAR is plausible (e.g., collapse periods have fewer records), Heckman selection correction or conservative worst-case imputation provides bounding cases.

C.2 Multiple Imputation and Uncertainty Propagation

The Seshat team assigns a distribution of possible values to each missing entry and samples 20 replicates. The imputation is performed on each replicate, producing 20 different sets of complexity characteristics. The historical tube is the union of the resulting trajectories:

\mathcal{T}_H = \bigcup_{m=1}^{20} \mathcal{T}_H^{(m)}.

Uncertainty propagates through PCA, dynamical estimation, viability computation, and TDA. The persistence diagram is computed on each implicate, and the bottleneck distance between diagrams computed on different implicates quantifies imputation sensitivity.

C.3 Temporal Resolution and Nyquist Aliasing

With century resolution, the Nyquist limit is 200 years. Fast elite cycles (duration < 100 years) are aliased. Simulation-based calibration generates synthetic data from a known dynamical system with the same sampling and missingness structure, attempts recovery, and reports recovery error versus noise. If recovery fails on synthetic data, the empirical result is unreliable.

Appendix D: TDA Null Models and Stability

D.1 Pre-Registered Null Models

Four null models are used: (1) matched-density Poisson process on the same latent domain; (2) block bootstrap preserving temporal autocorrelation; (3) surrogate trajectories from the estimated dynamics \hat{F} with matched noise and constraints; (4) phase-randomized surrogates preserving the autocorrelation structure of x(t). Significance is assessed via bootstrap permutation tests.

D.2 Metric Sensitivity and Bottleneck Stability

The persistence diagram is computed for a family of metrics g^{(\alpha)} parameterized by scaling weight, whitening (full covariance versus diagonal), and temporal weighting. The maximum bottleneck distance across the metric family must not exceed 0.1 \times \text{median persistence lifespan}. If it does, the topological conclusion fails robustness.

D.3 Topology-to-Dynamics Inference Warning

A persistent H_1 feature means that the data contain a robust loop-like structure under the chosen filtration. It does not automatically imply a stable periodic orbit. The mapping from topology to dynamics is established through the estimated dynamics \hat{F}, not assumed. The correct architecture is:

\text{Topology} + \text{Dynamics} \rightarrow \text{Civilizational Interpretation}.

Appendix E: Normative Operationalization of H_{\text{auth}}

E.1 Is-Ought Separation and the Normative Register

The is–ought gap establishes that normative statements cannot be logically derived from descriptive premises. The constraint H_{\text{auth}}(t) \ge H_{\text{crit}} is a chosen boundary condition, not a theorem. It is exogenous to the formal and empirical registers.

E.2 Measurable Proxies for Human Authority

Operationalize H_{\text{auth}} as the fraction of executive decisions requiring human signature, or the human override rate in AI-assisted governance. Without operationalization, the constraint cannot be varied in sensitivity analysis. The meaningful human control literature emphasizes that human involvement must connect relevant human reasons to system behavior and provide real capacity to prevent, redirect, or repair action.

E.3 Sensitivity Analysis over H_{\text{crit}}

Different values of H_{\text{crit}} carve different viability kernels. Report the volume of \mathrm{Viab}(\mathcal{S}_{\text{viable}}) as a function of H_{\text{crit}}. The volume ratio \mathrm{Vol}(\Omega_{\text{reachable}})/\mathrm{Vol}(\Omega_{\text{viable}}) is computed via Monte Carlo volume estimation for each H_{\text{crit}}.

Appendix F: Hierarchical Bayesian Calibration of \tau^*

F.1 Model Specification

The trust threshold is domain-specific:

\tau^*_{\text{domain}} \sim \text{Normal}(\mu_\tau, \sigma_\tau), \quad \mu_\tau \sim \text{Beta}(7,3).

The prior is centered near 0.7 but with wide variance. Calibration uses Seshat data and contemporary trust surveys (World Values Survey, Edelman Trust Barometer). Posterior overlap with 0.5 or 1.0 constitutes falsification.

F.2 Falsification Conditions

If the 95% credible interval for \tau^*_{\text{domain}} includes 0.5 or 1.0, the threshold hypothesis is rejected for that domain. If the posterior is unimodal and concentrated near 0.7 across all domains, the domain-specificity claim is weakened.

Appendix G: Simulation-Based Calibration for Dynamical Estimation

G.1 Synthetic Data Generation

Generate synthetic trajectories from a known dynamical system F with the same sampling frequency, missingness pattern, and noise level as Seshat. Attempt recovery using Gaussian Process regression, SINDy, and Neural ODE. Report recovery error as a function of noise level and missingness fraction.

G.2 Recovery Error and Aliasing

If recovery error exceeds 20% of the true coefficient magnitude, the empirical estimate is unreliable. If the true dynamics contain cycles with period < 200 years, the recovery will systematically fail due to aliasing. State this limitation explicitly and report the Nyquist limit.

